the arrival of the BMW 1500 in 1961  marked the start of a new era for the  Bavarian car maker classic car experts  gustav bauer takes us on a trip back in  time he says that BMW was in crisis in  the early 1950s and was even in danger  of being taken over by archrival  Mercedes that's because apart from  motorbikes BMW produced just two kinds  of vehicles small cars and luxury sedans  or as de Spiegel magazine put it cars  for laborers and CEOs so the 1500 was  aimed at the middle classes it was the  first of what became known as BMWs new  class to make the new class palatable to  the middle classes BMW used just the  right ingredients to make it practical  the 1500 had four doors its  four-cylinder engine was thrifty but  still delivered 80 horsepower and at a  price of 8500 dorch marks it made many a  family man's mouth water christophe says  the BMW 1500 made its debut at the  Frankfurt Motor Show in 1961 it thrilled  the crowds and around 2,000 orders were  taken than in there but BMW took its  time putting the model into series  production when the association of the  automotive industry threatened to  penalize BMW it took 35 pre-production  models put them on the assembly line for  another model and got some mechanics to  look like they were tinkering around  with the car then they said look mr.  inspector we're building it and it  worked which was a good thing as with  the new class BMW was one of the first  European car makers to venture into new  automotive terrain the modern  four-cylinder engine used in the 1500  would be fitted into other BMW models  for the next 25 years  the 1500 came to define what BMW stood  for as a brand for now listen Victor  Kristoph says the car looks small and  refined from the outside but inside it's  quite spacious which was by no means a  given for a car of its class in the  1960s but Christoph says it's a matter  of geometry experts know that a cube  boasts more volume than a sphere with  the same surface area and BMWs head  designer Vilhelm Hofmeister used this to  his advantage he defined the basic  features of BMWs design language  including the famous Hofmeister kink the  firm still uses today the characteristic  kidney radiator grille was the only  homage to BMWs glorious past otherwise  the 1500 broke with tradition completely  the cars delicate and almost rectangular  roof lets in plenty of light the sharp  angles and side seams were a novelty at  the time but continue to add Verve to  automotive design to this day the foam  upholstery in the middle of the steering  wheel was an early concession to passive  safety just like the sunken instruments  which don't reflect in the windshield  the new class offered drivers and  passengers lots of room in a relatively  small space and kristef says the 1500's  four-cylinder powerplant is the stuff of  legend it has a cast-iron engine block  with an aluminum cylinder head and  overhead camshaft and a forged  crankshaft it's engineering wrote  automotive history in the 1980s some BMW  design engineers took a couple of these  engines with a service life of over  100,000 kilometers fitted them with a  turbocharger and sent them onto the  Formula One circuits of this world with  as much as 1,300 horsepower under the  hood it sounds absurd but it was enough  to win the 1983 Formula One World  Championships  this modest-looking engine ranks among  the most legendary in the history of  motorsport never in his wildest dreams  could developer Alexander from Falcon  housen have imagined that his innovation  would end up writing Formula One history  with the help of the m10 engine Nelson  Piquet took the 1983 driver's  championship his Brabham BMW is still  considered to be one of the most  powerful ever seen in Formula One  the four-speed transmission shifts  crisply and the suspension is well tuned  in the early 60s the BMW 1500 even gave  Porsches a run for their money  christos says it might not sound like  much today but at the time eking 80  horsepower out of 4 cylinders was like  declaring war on the competition  Porsches base model 356 had just 60  horsepower the 1500 was called a  sprinter in a tux and Christoph thinks  Mattson apt description he says this 1.5  liter engine is very responsive but at  4,500 rpms it's pretty loud and sounds a  little labored but BMW already had  improvements in the works later new  class models like the BMW 2002tii with  up to two liters of cubic capacity and  130 horsepower helped drivers such as  hoover tannerite touring car history BMW  produced the model range for a decade  selling over 360,000 vehicles  Christophe says there's no question that  as the first midsize BMW of the post-war  era the new class route automotive  history and almost single-handedly saved  the company from bankruptcy even today  folks at BMW are proud of this car's  most important feature how fun it is to  drive so Christoph says the BMW 1500 is  definitely a milestone in automotive  history you could say that the BMW 1500  fill the gap left by the borg vard  isabella it's modern design was inspired  by the American Chevrolet Corvair and  numerous Italian models but in its day  the BMW 1500 was pretty much in a class  all its own  you
 

